MUSIC LIBRARY PROJECT — STEP-BY-STEP GUIDE
========================================

This guide turns your music_library.py requests into chronological steps.
Follow them in order to build the project incrementally. Later steps replace
some earlier approaches, such as converting getter methods into properties
and consolidating standalone examples into run_demo().

STEP 1 — Create the Track class
Create music_library.py and define a class named Track.
Add an __init__() method that accepts title (string), artist (string), and
duration (integer in seconds). Store each parameter as an instance attribute
using self.

STEP 2 — Create and inspect a sample track
Below the class definition, with proper spacing, create sample_track:
  Title: Bohemian Rhapsody
  Artist: Queen
  Duration: 354 seconds
Print each attribute using dot notation to verify the object was created.

STEP 3 — Add playback behavior
Define play() in Track. Use an f-string to print:
  Now playing: [title] by [artist]

STEP 4 — Return formatted track information
Define get_info() to return, rather than print, the title, artist, and duration.
Convert the duration from seconds into minutes and seconds (M:SS).
Example: Bohemian Rhapsody by Queen (5:54).

STEP 5 — Allow duration extensions
Define extend_duration(extra_seconds), accepting an integer.
Add extra_seconds to the track's duration and print a confirmation showing
the new total duration.

STEP 6 — Test the initial methods
Create a Track for Stairway to Heaven by Led Zeppelin, lasting 482 seconds.
Call play(), print get_info(), call extend_duration(10), and print get_info()
again. Verify the duration changes from 8:02 to 8:12.

STEP 7 — Count plays across all tracks
Add total_plays = 0 at the class level, before __init__().
Update play() to increment Track.total_plays by one whenever any track plays.

STEP 8 — Count plays for each individual track
Initialize self.play_count to zero in __init__().
Update play() to increment self.play_count as well as Track.total_plays.

STEP 9 — Verify individual and shared play counts
Create two different tracks. Play the first three times and the second twice.
Print each track's play_count and Track.total_plays.
Verify the individual counts are 3 and 2, and the total is their sum, 5.

STEP 10 — Use protected attribute naming
Rename title, artist, and duration storage to _title, _artist, and _duration.
Update all methods that reference these attributes.
A single underscore indicates nonpublic data by convention.

STEP 11 — Validate constructor input
Before assigning attributes in __init__(), raise ValueError with a descriptive
message if the title is empty, the artist is empty, or duration is zero or less.

STEP 12 — Add getter methods
Define get_title(), get_artist(), and get_duration().
Each method returns the corresponding protected attribute value.

STEP 13 — Test invalid constructor arguments
At the bottom of music_library.py, add separate try-except blocks for:
  - An empty title.
  - An empty artist.
  - A duration of zero.
Catch ValueError in each case and print the error message.

STEP 14 — Convert getters into properties
Replace get_title(), get_artist(), and get_duration() with properties using
@property. Name the properties title, artist, and duration.
Access them like attributes, without parentheses.

STEP 15 — Add a validated duration setter
Add @duration.setter to allow duration changes only when the new value is
positive. For invalid values, raise ValueError with this exact message:
  Duration must be positive

STEP 16 — Add a formatted duration property
Create a read-only duration_formatted property that returns M:SS.
For example, 354 seconds should return "5:54". Do not add a setter.

STEP 17 — Test property access and assignment
Create a Track for Hotel California by Eagles, lasting 390 seconds.
Access its properties without parentheses.
Set duration to 400, then attempt to set it to -10 inside a try-except block.
Read duration_formatted and verify it is "6:40" after the invalid assignment.

STEP 18 — Create the Song subclass
Define Song as a subclass of Track with an additional genre argument.
Call super().__init__(title, artist, duration), then assign self._genre.

STEP 19 — Add song-specific properties and playback
Add a genre property that returns _genre.
Override Song.play() to print:
  Now playing [genre] song: [title] by [artist]
Preserve the shared and individual play-count behavior.

STEP 20 — Create the Episode subclass
Define Episode as a subclass of Track with host and episode_number attributes.
Override play() to print:
  Now playing podcast: [title] - Episode [episode_number], hosted by [host]

STEP 21 — Compare parent and child behavior
Create these instances:
  Track: Sound Effect, Studio, 5 seconds.
  Song: Imagine, John Lennon, 183 seconds, genre Rock.
  Episode: Tech Talk, NPR, 1800 seconds, host Jane Smith, episode number 42.
Call play() on each and observe the different messages.
Access title and duration on the Song and Episode to verify inheritance.

STEP 22 — Create the Audiobook subclass
Define Audiobook as a subclass of Track.
Add narrator and chapter_count attributes, and initialize current_chapter to 1.
Add next_chapter() to advance only when not already at the final chapter.
Override play() to show the current chapter.

STEP 23 — Extend the parent's information method
Override get_info() in Song.
First call super().get_info(), then append the genre to the returned string.

STEP 24 — Test audiobook chapter navigation
Create an Audiobook with:
  Title: The Hobbit
  Artist: J.R.R. Tolkien
  Duration: 39600 seconds (11 hours)
  Narrator: Martin Freeman
  Chapter count: 19
Call play(), call next_chapter() twice, then call play() again.
Verify the displayed chapter changes from 1 to 3.

STEP 25 — Create the Playlist class
Define Playlist with a name attribute and an empty protected list, _tracks.
Add these methods:
  add_track(track): append a track to the list.
  get_total_duration(): return the sum of all track durations in seconds.
  play_all(): call play() on each track in the playlist.

STEP 26 — Demonstrate a mixed playlist
Create a playlist named My Favorites.
Add a Song, an Episode, and an Audiobook.
Call play_all() and observe how each type responds differently to play().
This demonstrates polymorphism.

STEP 27 — Format the playlist's total duration
Add a read-only total_duration_formatted property to Playlist.
Return the total duration in H:MM:SS format, padding minutes and seconds
to two digits.

STEP 28 — Add a readable Track representation
Define Track.__str__() to return:
  [title] by [artist] (M:SS)
Printing a Track object should display this human-readable description.

STEP 29 — Add a constructor-style Track representation
Define Track.__repr__() to return a string resembling valid Python code:
  Track('Bohemian Rhapsody', 'Queen', 354)
Use appropriate quoting for string arguments.

STEP 30 — Customize subclass representations
Override __str__() and __repr__() in Song, Episode, and Audiobook.
Include each subclass's specific attributes.
For example, Song.__repr__() should follow this structure:
  Song('title', 'artist', duration, 'genre')

STEP 31 — Test both representations for all track types
Create instances of Track, Song, Episode, and Audiobook.
For each object, use print(object) to see __str__(), then use
print(repr(object)) to see __repr__().

STEP 32 — Consolidate the examples into run_demo()
Create a function named run_demo() that exercises the library:
  - Create Track, Song, Episode, and Audiobook instances.
  - Access properties instead of directly accessing protected attributes.
  - Demonstrate the child classes' additional features.
  - Call play() on a mixed list of track types.
  - Print objects and their repr() representations.
  - Create and use a Playlist.
Include checks that the demonstrated methods and properties work correctly.

STEP 33 — Add a dedicated validation test function
Create test_validation() with these four tests:
  - Creating Track with an empty title raises ValueError.
  - Creating Track with an empty artist raises ValueError.
  - Creating Track with zero duration raises ValueError.
  - Assigning an invalid duration through the setter raises ValueError.
Each test must print PASSED or FAILED according to whether the expected
exception was raised.

STEP 34 — Document the code
Add docstrings to describe the module, classes, methods, and functions.
Add explanatory comments for the steps and concepts developed throughout
the project, including the earlier examples and validation exercises.

STEP 35 — Create this project guide
Collect all music_library.py project prompts and turn them into an ordered
text document that can be followed as a step-by-step exercise.

RUNNING THE COMPLETED PROJECT
=============================
Run the following command from the directory containing music_library.py:
  python3 music_library.py

The completed script calls run_demo() and test_validation() from its
if __name__ == "__main__" block.
